 ## Background
-The concept of multi-stage training in 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a response to the instability and inefficiency of optimizing dense point clouds from sparse or random initializations. Early works noted that joint training of complex components (like depth networks in forward-warping methods) from scratch leads to instability, necessitating initialization stages [S3]. Similarly, in mesh-based and volumetric variants, the need to balance local detail reconstruction with global structural consistency led to curriculum learning approaches [S1, unofficial][S2]. The "Coarse-to-Fine" paradigm is widely adopted in computer vision for radiance fields and point clouds to ensure that global geometry is established before fine details are added [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of progressive training in neural rendering predates 3D Gaussian Splatting, often appearing in NeRF variants as coarse-to-fine frequency learning or multi-resolution hashing [G]. In the context of 3DGS, the original paper [3DGS] introduced a single-stage optimization with periodic densification. However, subsequent works identified limitations in this approach, such as redundancy in feed-forward initialization [9138], instability in dynamic scenes [2], and poor volumetric reconstruction in medical imaging [12921]. The Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy [7173] and MeshGS [7448] explicitly structure the densification process to improve geometric alignment and convergence. The Two-Stage Training for FWD-U [3] highlights the necessity of decoupling depth estimation from view synthesis to avoid optimization instability. These methods collectively form the "Progressive and Multi-stage Training" family, addressing the need for structured optimization in complex radiance field representations.
 
